(* ============================================================================
  10. A LIST ITEM'S CONTINUATION LINES ARE ALIGNED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-027]. Every
      continuation line of a list item is indented to the WIDTH OF THE MARKER,
      including one whose text would otherwise read as a list marker. Such a
      line is kept literal by ESCAPING it, per §2, not by indenting it less:

        1. outer
           1\. inner

      This does not conflict with §2. §2 governs whether a character is escaped
      GIVEN the line the writer emits; §10 fixes the line. A writer may not
      manufacture a layout whose only merit is dodging an escape.

  10b. A TABLE ROW KEEPS ITS OWN CELL COUNT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-029]. The canonical
      writer emits exactly the cells the row carries. It MAY align the padding
      of cells that exist, but MUST NOT append empty cells to make every row as
      wide as the widest row in the table. A missing trailing cell is not an
      empty cell: adding one changes both the re-parsed tree and the rendered
      table, so CARVE-P11-002 makes rectangular alignment yield.

      A SEPARATOR ROW IS WRITTEN ONLY WHERE THE TREE RECORDS ONE. Its cell
      count is the entry count of `table.separatorWidths` (CARVE-P11-066),
      neither the header row's width nor the table's. Without the field the
      header is written with native `=` markers, including when a later body
      row is wider.

  10c. ADJACENT BLOCKS THAT WOULD MERGE KEEP THE CONTINUATION MARKER --
       NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-030]. In a tight list item, deciding whether to retain `+` is a
       property of the emitted BLOCK SEQUENCE, not only of each child in
       isolation. If two adjacent children written at the item's content
       column would re-parse as one block, the writer emits each child from the
       first such child onward at the item's marker column, preceded by its
       own `+` when required for the round-trip contract.
       In a nested tight item, the writer first writes the block at the item's
       content column with a blank line before it; L2 keeps the item tight.
       It MAY emit a `+` at that nested item's marker column only when the
       blank-line spelling changes the parsed tree, including its tightness or
       the block's owner (markup-carve/carve#3421). If no spelling round-trips,
       the writer reports a writer loss.

       This includes adjacent block quotes, tables, line blocks, definition
       lists, and lists whose marker kind is compatible. A single block opener
       that cannot fold into the paragraph above it remains canonically
       indented at the content column. In a nested item, the blank line is
       the compact-list separator specified by L2.


  10f. A MARKDOWN IMPORT READS AN ABBREVIATION DEFINITION LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-033].
      A `*[TERM]: expansion` line in Markdown input is an abbreviation
      definition, not a paragraph: it is the spelling PHP Markdown Extra
      uses for an abbreviation. An `<abbr>` whose title is the expansion a
      definition in the same document gives its text imports as the plain
      term that definition resolves. No carrier is needed.

  10g. A COMPOSITE FIGURE SURVIVES EVERY TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-034]
      (CARVE-P9-001). The floor is `docs/graceful-degradation.md`'s: panel
      content, panel captions, preserved stray content and the group caption
      are all CONTENT, and no target may silently discard any of them. What
      this clause decides is the canonical writer's spelling. The Markdown,
      plain-text and terminal renderings are writer guidance
      (`docs/writer-targets.md`).

      THE CANONICAL WRITER EMITS THE AUTHORED FORM: the attribute line where
      attributes exist, the bare `::: figure` opener (`figure_group` has no
      title or label to spell), the children with one blank line between
      them, the closer at the opener's width (the inward-widening discipline
      of PART 2 `colon_fence_close` unchanged), and the group caption as a `^ ` line after
      the closer, its `#` placeholder written back as authored.

      THE CARET AFTER A GROUP CLOSER IS A CAPTION POSITION. §5's
      unconditional set escapes a leading caret in TEXT; the group caption is
      NOT text in that position -- it is the caption the closer hosts -- so
      the writer MUST NOT emit it as `\^ `, which would re-parse as a
      paragraph and break §1's `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)`. The other
      direction holds too: a PARAGRAPH whose text begins with a caret,
      sitting where PART 9 §4's allowance reaches a group closer above it, keeps its
      `\^ ` escape, because bare it would re-parse as the group caption
      (corpus 318-composite-figures-6 holds the detached shape).

      Tabs, `presentation=` hints and PDF layout are renderer/extension
      business and out of this clause's scope; hint classes reach the HTML
      target only.

  10i. A HARD LIST BOUNDARY IS WRITTEN AS EXACTLY THREE BLANK LINES --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-036]. The writer normalizes a run of blank lines between two blocks
      to ONE. A run that is PART 9 §11 N1a's hard list boundary is the
      exception, and it is written as EXACTLY THREE -- not one, and not the run
      the author happened to type:

        - apples                 is written as:   - apples
        (six blank lines)                         (three blank lines)
        - oranges                                 - oranges

      One blank line would merge the lists, violating §1. PART 9 §11 N1a reads THREE
      OR MORE, so three is the canonical form for every hard-boundary run.

      A RUN THAT IS NOT THE BOUNDARY NORMALIZES AS USUAL. Three blank lines
      between two paragraphs, or between two lists that already differ on a PART 9 §11 N1
      axis (`- a` then `* b`), separate nothing that one blank line does not
      separate as well, so they come back as one. What earns the three is the
      boundary's job, not the run's length.

  10j. AN UNSPELLABLE BLOCK DOES NOT CANCEL THE ADJACENCY IT CANNOT SPELL --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-037]. Where EVERY block between two sibling lists is
      UNSPELLABLE -- it reaches the writer from the AST and leaves no character
      on the page -- the two lists are ADJACENT for CARVE-P11-036's purposes, and
      CARVE-P11-036 decides the run between them as if nothing had stood there.

      A whitespace-only paragraph has no Carve spelling, so it cannot separate
      the emitted lists. Without this rule they return with one blank line and
      merge into one loose list, violating §1.

      IT IS NOT A PARAGRAPH RULE. Any INTERCHANGE-ONLY block has this shape: a
      `figure` wrapping a `table` (PART 12 §17) is unspellable in the same
      sense and would cancel the same adjacency. So the rule is stated over what
      a block SPELLS and never over its type, and it reaches every such shape a
      later clause admits without naming one of them. A block that spells
      ANYTHING -- a thematic break, or a paragraph holding a NO-BREAK SPACE
      (§7) -- separates the two lists as it always did, and no boundary is
      written.

      NO CARVE SOURCE REACHES THIS: the shape enters through the AST ingest.

  10k. THE ROUND-TRIP COMPARISON NORMALIZES A NAMED LIST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-043].
      A round trip preserves MEANING, not one serialization. Two renderings of
      a document compare equal when they differ only by an entry on this list,
      which is CLOSED:

      N1 `<del>` and `<s>` are one tag.
      N2 A nested emphasis of DIFFERENT strengths whose child spans the whole
         parent may commute, so `<strong><em>x</em></strong>` and
         `<em><strong>x</strong></em>` are one document.
      N3 An EMPTY delimited comment compares equal to nothing, so a producer
         may separate two constructs that would otherwise merge with an empty
         `{% ... %}`, which is how two adjacent code spans are kept apart.
         Where an escape separates them, the escape is written instead: it is
         what §2 already emits and it leaves no node (carve#2068).

      Everything else is byte-exact. EQUAL strengths are not on the list:
      `<em><em>x</em></em>` against `<strong>x</strong>` is an italic turned
      bold, which is a different document.

      THE LIST IS ENUMERATED AND MECHANICAL, never a judgment about what means
      the same. The defects this comparison has found were found because it was
      exact, and an open-ended equivalence would hide the next one. An entry is
      written here or it does not exist.

  10n. A HEADER ROW IS A ROW OF HEADER CELLS, AND A MARKDOWN IMPORT OMITS AN
      EMPTY ONE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-056].
      A ROW IS A HEADER ROW WHEN EVERY CELL IT OWNS IS A HEADER CELL. A span
      placeholder (a cell carrying `span`) is not a cell of its own: it
      belongs to the cell whose `colspan` or `rowspan` covers it, so
      `|= A | < |` is a header row even though its placeholder carries
      `header: false`.

      ON MARKDOWN IMPORT, an all-empty header with no column alignment is
      omitted, together with its delimiter row. It is the scaffolding the
      Markdown target writes for a table with no header row
      (`docs/writer-targets.md`), so keeping it would give every headerless
      table an empty header row on its way back. The body keeps its GFM column width,
      and the importer MUST NOT turn the omitted header into a paragraph of
      pipes. A header carrying cell content or column alignment is retained.
      The omitted header is reported as `structure-unspellable` by the
      migration fidelity report; a standalone GFM empty header cannot be
      distinguished from writer scaffolding. An all-blank BODY row is not
      scaffolding: it is imported as a row of empty cells, one `| |` slot
      per cell, and reads back as a row (PART 9 §5 CARVE-P9-082).

   ============================================================================ *)
